// Marginalia reference data, loaded by index.html before the app. Plain JavaScript so it keeps its comments.
const BRANCHES = [
  // by subject
  { id: "metaphysics", n: "Metaphysics", k: "branch", d: "The study of what there is and what it is like at the most general level: being, substance, identity, causation, time and possibility. Ontology, the theory of being, is its core.", q: ["What exists, and in what ways?", "What makes a thing the same thing over time?", "Is everything that happens determined?"], p: ["parmenides", "aristotle", "ibnsina", "spinoza", "leibniz", "hegel", "heidegger"], st: ["Aristotle, Metaphysics", "Descartes, Meditations on First Philosophy (1641)", "Spinoza, Ethics (1677)"], rel: ["epistemology", "mind", "religion", "logic"], x: "The name comes from a book title. Aristotle never used the word; he called the inquiry first philosophy, first science, wisdom or theology. An ancient editor, probably Andronicus of Rhodes, grouped fourteen of his books under a title meaning roughly “the ones after the physical ones”, likely because they were to be studied after the Physics. Aristotle describes their subject in two ways, as being as such and as first causes, and how the two descriptions fit together is still debated.\n\nFrom the seventeenth century the field widened to take in problems that fitted nowhere else: mind and body, free will, personal identity. The word “ontology” was coined in this period for the study of being as such, and Christian Wolff split metaphysics into general metaphysics (ontology) and special metaphysics, which treated particular kinds of being such as the soul, the world and God.\n\nToday its main topics include existence and non-being, universals and particulars (realism against nominalism), substance, necessity and possibility (possible worlds), the nature of time, how things persist through change, causation and free will, the relation of mind and body, and the nature of social kinds such as money, race and gender. Hume and the logical positivists argued that metaphysical questions are unanswerable or meaningless, and Kant argued that reason overreaches when it claims knowledge beyond possible experience. Analytic philosophy nonetheless saw a strong revival of metaphysics in the last third of the twentieth century, with new work on grounding and fundamentality.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/metaphysics/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" },
  { id: "epistemology", n: "Epistemology", k: "branch", d: "The study of knowledge and justified belief: what knowledge is, where it comes from, and how far it reaches.", q: ["What is knowledge, and how does it differ from true belief?", "Can anything be known with certainty?", "What do experience and reason each contribute?"], p: ["plato", "descartes", "locke", "hume", "kant", "quine", "harding", "fricker"], st: ["Plato, Theaetetus", "Hume, An Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding (1748)", "Kant, Critique of Pure Reason (1781)"], rel: ["metaphysics", "science", "logic", "feminist"], x: "Epistemology studies cognitive success and failure: knowing, understanding, believing with good reason, and their opposites. Its central concept has long been knowledge that something is the case, which differs from knowing a person or knowing how to do something.\n\nThe traditional analysis treated knowledge as justified true belief. Edmund Gettier's three-page paper of 1963 described cases in which someone has a justified true belief that is true only by luck, and so does not seem to know. Responses include adding a fourth condition, accounts built on intellectual virtues, and treating knowledge as basic and unanalysable. Debates about justification divide evidentialists, who tie it to the evidence a person has, from reliabilists, who tie it to reliable belief-forming processes; internalists hold that justification depends only on what is within the believer's own perspective, and externalists deny this. On the structure of justification, foundationalists hold that some beliefs are basic and support the rest, while coherentists hold that beliefs support one another as a web.\n\nThe traditional sources of knowledge are perception, introspection, memory, reason and testimony, the last raising the question of when we may rely on what others tell us. Skeptical arguments, such as the possibility of being a brain in a vat, ask whether any of these sources can deliver knowledge at all. Newer fields include formal epistemology, which models degrees of belief with probability, social epistemology, and feminist epistemology, which studies how a knower's social position and the practices of judging others shape what can be known.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/epistemology/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" },
  { id: "logic", n: "Logic", k: "branch", d: "The study of correct reasoning: which inferences preserve truth, and why. Modern logic studies formal systems as well as arguments in ordinary language.", q: ["What makes an argument valid?", "What is the logical form of a statement?", "How do logic and language relate?"], p: ["aristotle", "ockham", "frege", "russell", "godel"], st: ["Aristotle, Prior Analytics", "Frege, Begriffsschrift (1879)", "Russell, “On Denoting” (1905)"], rel: ["mathematics", "language", "analytic"], x: "Logic studies correct reasoning, usually in the form of arguments: premises offered in support of a conclusion. Formal logic examines the structure of arguments in symbolic languages, independent of their subject matter. Informal logic analyses arguments in ordinary language and studies fallacies, critical thinking and argumentation.\n\nA deductively valid argument is one whose conclusion must be true if its premises are; validity depends on form, and a valid argument with true premises is sound. Ampliative arguments add information their premises do not contain and make their conclusions probable at best. Induction generalizes from observed cases; abduction infers the best explanation of the evidence, as a doctor does in diagnosing a disease. Fallacies are faulty arguments, formal when the fault is in the structure, informal when it lies in content or context, such as ambiguity or irrelevance.\n\nAristotle's syllogistic was the dominant Western system for about two thousand years, alongside Stoic, Indian, Chinese, medieval and Arabic traditions of logic. Modern formal logic arose in the late nineteenth century with Gottlob Frege and others. Classical logic, today's standard system, comprises propositional and first-order logic. Extended logics, such as modal, deontic and epistemic logic, apply it to necessity, obligation and knowledge; deviant logics, such as intuitionistic, many-valued and paraconsistent logic, give up some of its principles.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Logic", srcName: "Wikipedia" },
  { id: "ethics", n: "Ethics", k: "branch", d: "The study of how to live and act: what is good, what is right, and what we owe one another. It divides into metaethics, normative ethics and applied ethics.", q: ["What makes an action right or wrong?", "What is a good life?", "Are there moral facts?"], p: ["aristotle", "kant", "mill", "nietzsche", "beauvoir", "anscombe"], st: ["Aristotle, Nicomachean Ethics", "Kant, Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals (1785)", "Mill, Utilitarianism (1861)", "Nietzsche, On the Genealogy of Morality (1887)"], rel: ["political", "religion", "law", "chinese"], x: "Ethics, or moral philosophy, asks what people ought to do, which conduct is right, and how to live well. It has three branches: normative ethics seeks general principles of right action, applied ethics examines concrete questions such as abortion, the treatment of animals or business practice, and metaethics asks about the nature of morality itself.\n\nThe main normative theories differ over what matters most. Consequentialism holds that an act is right when it produces the best outcome; classical utilitarianism, developed by Bentham and Mill, takes happiness to be the good to be maximized. Deontology holds that some acts are required or forbidden whatever their consequences, such as keeping promises and telling the truth; Kant grounded morality in universal principles and in treating persons as ends, never merely as means. Virtue ethics centers on character, and Aristotle described each virtue as a mean between excess and deficiency. Other views include divine command theory, contractualism, discourse ethics and ethical egoism.\n\nMetaethics asks whether there are objective moral facts (moral realism) or whether morality is relative or illusory, whether moral statements can be true or false (cognitivism) or instead express attitudes or commands (non-cognitivism, including emotivism and prescriptivism), and how moral knowledge would be possible. Ethical thought has deep roots outside Greece: in the Egyptian idea of maat, in Indian traditions from the Vedas to Buddhism, and in Confucian and Daoist China.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ethics", srcName: "Wikipedia" },
  { id: "aesthetics", n: "Aesthetics", k: "branch", d: "The study of beauty, art and taste: what art is, how aesthetic judgment works, and what art does in social life.", q: ["What makes something a work of art?", "Can judgments of taste be correct?", "How is art shaped by its social and technical conditions?"], p: ["burke", "kant", "hegel", "benjamin", "adorno", "lyotard"], st: ["Kant, Critique of the Power of Judgment (1790)", "Benjamin, “The Work of Art in the Age of Its Technological Reproducibility” (1935–36)", "Adorno, Aesthetic Theory (1970)"], rel: ["critical-theory", "phenomenology"], x: "The word “aesthetic” entered European philosophy in the eighteenth century, and it now names an object, a judgment, an attitude, an experience and a kind of value. It grew out of the concept of taste, developed partly against rationalist accounts of beauty. Dubos, Hume and Reid held that judgments of beauty are immediate, like judgments of the senses: reasoning may be needed to take in a complex work, but the verdict itself is not inferred.\n\nHutcheson and Hume held that pleasure in beauty is disinterested, and Kant made the idea central: a judgment of taste is contemplative and indifferent to whether its object serves any desire, yet it claims everyone's agreement. Formalists such as Hanslick, Clive Bell and Clement Greenberg held that only a work's formal properties matter to its value as art. Arthur Danto challenged this with objects that look identical to ordinary things, such as Warhol's Brillo Boxes, and Kendall Walton argued that the category under which we perceive a work changes which aesthetic properties it has.\n\nTwentieth-century debates asked whether aesthetic judgments follow general principles (Beardsley) or respond to particulars (Sibley, Isenberg); whether a special aesthetic attitude of disinterested attention exists (Schopenhauer, Bullough, Stolnitz), which George Dickie's critique did much to undermine; and whether aesthetic experience is defined by its inner character (Dewey, early Beardsley) or by the features of what is experienced. A parallel tradition, running from Benjamin to Adorno, asks what art does in social and political life.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aesthetic-concept/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" },
  { id: "political", n: "Political and social philosophy", k: "branch", d: "The study of power, authority, justice and freedom, and of the social structures that shape people's lives: what makes rule legitimate, and what a just society would be.", q: ["What makes political authority legitimate?", "What does justice require?", "How do social structures produce and constrain persons?"], p: ["plato", "hobbes", "locke", "rousseau", "marx", "schmitt", "arendt", "rawls"], st: ["Plato, Republic", "Hobbes, Leviathan (1651)", "Rousseau, The Social Contract (1762)", "Rawls, A Theory of Justice (1971)"], rel: ["ethics", "law", "marxism", "critical-theory", "race"], x: "Political philosophy studies the foundations of political life: the nature and legitimacy of the state, the forms government can take, and the values that should guide political action. Political science describes how governments work; political philosophy asks how they should.\n\nIts central concepts include power, which can rest on consent or on coercion; legitimacy, the question whether power is rightly acquired and used; authority, the right to rule; justice in the treatment of persons and the distribution of goods; equality, of rights, of opportunity or of outcomes; and liberty, often divided into negative liberty, freedom from interference, and positive liberty, the power to act. Methods differ too: some theorists build up from particular judgments, others start from first principles, and Rawls's reflective equilibrium seeks a balance between the two.\n\nPlato and Aristotle asked about justice and the best state, while Confucians, Daoists and Legalists debated government in China, and Hindu and Buddhist political thought developed in India. Medieval Christian and Islamic thinkers joined Greek ideas to religious traditions. Machiavelli, Hobbes, Locke and Rousseau gave secular accounts of the state, many of them through the social contract. In the nineteenth century Marx, Mill and Tocqueville shaped modern debate, and in the twentieth Rawls and Nozick revived the field. Its major schools include liberalism, conservatism, socialism and Marxism, anarchism, republicanism, communitarianism and feminism.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Political_philosophy", srcName: "Wikipedia" },
  { id: "mind", n: "Philosophy of mind", k: "branch", d: "The study of the mind and consciousness, and of their relation to the body and the physical world.", q: ["How is the mind related to the body?", "What is consciousness?", "How can we know other minds?"], p: ["descartes", "elisabeth", "brentano", "ryle", "nagel"], st: ["Descartes, Meditations on First Philosophy (1641)", "Ryle, The Concept of Mind (1949)", "Nagel, “What Is It Like to Be a Bat?” (1974)"], rel: ["metaphysics", "language", "phenomenology"], x: "Philosophy of mind centers on the mind-body problem: how minds relate to bodies and to the rest of the world. Descartes held that the mind is a non-physical thinking substance that interacts with the body. Leibniz proposed instead that mind and body run in a harmony established in advance, Malebranche and al-Ghazālī that God brings about each apparent interaction, and Spinoza that mind and matter are two aspects of one substance. Idealists hold that only mind exists; physicalists that only the physical does.\n\nTwentieth-century physicalist theories include behaviorism, the identity theory of Place and Smart (mental states are brain states), functionalism (mental states are defined by their causal roles, so the same mind could in principle run on different hardware), Davidson's anomalous monism, and the eliminative materialism of the Churchlands, who expect everyday psychological concepts to be abandoned. Property dualists accept only physical substance but hold that mental properties cannot be reduced to physical ones.\n\nThe hardest problems are subjective experience, the “what it is like” of seeing red or feeling pain, and intentionality, the way thoughts are about things. Chalmers's zombie argument holds that a being physically identical to us but without experience is conceivable, and therefore that physicalism is false; Dennett is among those who reject it. Searle's Chinese Room asks whether running a program could ever amount to understanding. The field also takes in free will and personal identity, and draws on neuroscience and cognitive science, as well as Indian traditions such as Sāṃkhya and Madhyamaka Buddhism and the phenomenology of Husserl and Heidegger.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philosophy_of_mind", srcName: "Wikipedia" },
  { id: "language", n: "Philosophy of language", k: "branch", d: "The study of meaning, reference and truth, and of what people do with words.", q: ["How do words come to mean anything?", "How do names refer to things?", "What do we do when we speak?"], p: ["frege", "saussure", "wittgenstein", "austin", "kripke"], st: ["Frege, “On Sense and Reference” (1892)", "Wittgenstein, Philosophical Investigations (1953)", "Austin, How to Do Things with Words (1962)"], rel: ["logic", "mind", "analytic", "poststructuralism"], x: "Modern philosophy of language begins with Frege, who distinguished an expression's reference, the thing it picks out, from its sense, the way that thing is presented. The distinction explains why “the morning star is the evening star” can be informative though both names refer to Venus. Russell's “On Denoting” (1905) analyzed phrases like “the present King of France” as claims of existence and uniqueness, so that a sentence about something that does not exist can be meaningful and false.\n\nWittgenstein's Tractatus (1921) held that propositions picture facts, and the Vienna Circle's verificationism counted a statement meaningful only if it is true by definition or can be checked by experience, which ruled out much of metaphysics and ethics. Tarski gave a rigorous definition of truth for formal languages. Quine attacked the distinction between analytic and synthetic truths and argued that translation is indeterminate. The later Wittgenstein of the Philosophical Investigations (1953) tied meaning to use within “language games” embedded in social practice, and argued that a private language is impossible.\n\nLater work includes truth-conditional semantics (Davidson, Lewis); the causal theory of reference, on which Kripke and Putnam held that names and natural-kind terms refer through a chain of use going back to an initial naming, leading to Putnam's claim that meanings are not in the head; inferentialist accounts of meaning (Sellars, Brandom); and speech act theory, in which Austin and Searle treated utterances as actions, while Grice explained how speakers convey more than they literally say. Continental approaches to language, from Saussure to Derrida, appear under structuralism and post-structuralism.", src: "https://iep.utm.edu/lang-phi/", srcName: "Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy" },
  { id: "science", n: "Philosophy of science", k: "branch", d: "The study of what science is, how scientific theories are tested and confirmed, and how science changes over time.", q: ["What separates science from non-science?", "How does evidence support a theory?", "Does science progress toward truth?"], p: ["bacon", "hume", "popper", "kuhn", "haraway"], st: ["Popper, The Logic of Scientific Discovery (1934)", "Kuhn, The Structure of Scientific Revolutions (1962)", "Haraway, “Situated Knowledges” (1988)"], rel: ["epistemology", "logic", "mathematics"], x: "Philosophy of science asks what distinguishes science from other kinds of inquiry, how evidence supports theories, and what science achieves. The logical positivists tied scientific meaning to observation; Karl Popper made falsifiability the mark of science, so that a theory counts as scientific if some possible observation could refute it. Some philosophers doubt that any sharp line between science and non-science can be drawn.\n\nHume's problem of induction, how past observations can justify claims about the unobserved, remains unresolved. One response treats evidence as raising a hypothesis's probability; another stresses inference to the best explanation. Hempel's deductive-nomological model treated explanation as deriving a phenomenon from laws, and met many counterexamples; later accounts stress causal mechanisms or unification. The Duhem-Quine thesis holds that a theory is always tested together with auxiliary assumptions, so a failed prediction does not show which part is at fault; the anomalous orbit of Uranus, which led to the discovery of Neptune, is the classic case. Observation itself may be shaped by theory.\n\nThomas Kuhn's Structure of Scientific Revolutions (1962) described periods of normal science within a paradigm, broken by revolutions in which a paradigm is replaced, and argued that the choice between paradigms is partly social. Paul Feyerabend denied that any universal method exists. Realists hold that successful theories are approximately true; anti-realists point to the many successful theories later abandoned. Sociologists of knowledge, feminist philosophers of science, and continental thinkers such as Heidegger, Foucault and Habermas study science as a historical and social practice.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philosophy_of_science", srcName: "Wikipedia" },
  { id: "religion", n: "Philosophy of religion", k: "branch", d: "The rational examination of religious belief and practice: arguments about God's existence, the problem of evil, the sacred, and the relation of faith to reason.", q: ["Can God's existence be proven?", "How can a good God permit evil?", "What is the relation between faith and reason?"], p: ["augustine", "anselm", "aquinas", "ghazali", "hume", "kierkegaard"], st: ["Anselm, Proslogion", "al-Ghazālī, The Incoherence of the Philosophers", "Hume, Dialogues Concerning Natural Religion (1779)", "Kierkegaard, Fear and Trembling (1843)"], rel: ["metaphysics", "ethics", "islamic", "indian"], x: "Philosophy of religion examines religious traditions and wider questions of religious meaning: what religion is, what God might be, and what significance the cosmos and history might have. It draws on every major area of philosophy, and it has increasingly turned to non-theistic and non-Abrahamic traditions such as Buddhism, Hinduism and Daoism.\n\nIn the mid-twentieth century logical positivists such as A. J. Ayer judged religious claims meaningless because they cannot be verified by experience. Critics replied that the criterion of verification undermines itself and is too narrow, and philosophers influenced by Wittgenstein turned to how religious language functions within religious life. In religious epistemology, evidentialists require that belief in God be supported by evidence, while Alvin Plantinga's reformed epistemology holds that such belief can be warranted without argument. Further debates concern disagreement among equally reasonable people, and how science and religion relate.\n\nThe classic arguments for God's existence are the ontological argument, which Anselm drew from the concept of a being than which none greater can be conceived; cosmological arguments from the existence of the world to a first cause; teleological or design arguments from order in nature; and arguments from religious experience. Hume's Dialogues and Kant's first Critique contain the most famous criticisms. Other central topics are the divine attributes, such as omniscience and how it fits with human freedom, and the problem of evil: how a good and all-powerful God could permit suffering.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/philosophy-religion/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" },
  { id: "history", n: "Philosophy of history", k: "branch", d: "Reflection on whether history has a direction or meaning, and on how historical knowledge is possible.", q: ["Does history have a direction?", "What drives historical change?", "How should the past be written?"], p: ["hegel", "marx", "benjamin", "kojeve", "foucault", "fukuyama"], st: ["Hegel, Lectures on the Philosophy of History", "Benjamin, “On the Concept of History” (1940)", "Foucault, “Nietzsche, Genealogy, History” (1971)"], rel: ["marxism", "german-idealism", "poststructuralism"], x: "Philosophy of history asks what history consists of, whether it has a meaning or direction, how historians know and explain the past, and what moral responsibilities the past creates. Its speculative strand looked for large patterns. Vico held that a universal human nature shapes the course of nations, Herder that human nature is itself formed by history, Hegel that history is the unfolding of freedom, and Marx that it is driven by developments in production and class conflict. Critics object to the single causes these accounts propose and to finding purpose where no agent directs events.\n\nThe hermeneutic tradition separated the human sciences from the natural sciences. Dilthey held that the former rest on understanding meaningful action, the latter on causal explanation, and Collingwood proposed that historians know the past by re-enacting the thought of historical agents. Analytic philosophers debated Hempel's covering-law model of 1942, on which historical explanation derives events from general laws; Dray and others argued that history rarely works this way.\n\nFrom the 1970s Hayden White and Louis Mink turned attention to narrative and to the rhetorical form of historical writing, and Ricoeur linked memory, narrative, identity and history. Reinhart Koselleck's conceptual history studies how the concepts historians use change over time. Recent work emphasizes that the historian's choice of theme, place and scale shapes what can be known, that evidence underdetermines narrative so that several truthful accounts can coexist, and that Eurocentric frameworks distort the histories of other parts of the world.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/history/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" },
  { id: "law", n: "Philosophy of law", k: "branch", d: "The study of what law is, how it relates to morality, and what justifies punishment.", q: ["What makes a rule a law?", "Must law be just to be law?", "What justifies punishment?"], p: ["aquinas", "bentham", "johnaustin", "kelsen", "hart", "dworkin", "foucault"], st: ["Aquinas, Summa Theologiae I–II, qq. 90–97", "Hart, The Concept of Law (1961)", "Foucault, Discipline and Punish (1975)"], rel: ["political", "ethics"], x: "Philosophy of law, or jurisprudence, asks what law is and how it relates to morality. Legal positivists hold that whether a norm is legally valid depends only on social facts, such as whether it was enacted in the right way, and never on its moral merit. John Austin treated law as the command of a sovereign backed by threats. H. L. A. Hart objected that this cannot account for rules that confer powers, such as those for making wills or contracts, and replaced it with a system of primary rules of conduct and secondary rules, including a rule of recognition that officials accept from an internal point of view. Inclusive positivists allow morality into the test of validity when legal convention puts it there; exclusive positivists do not.\n\nNatural law theorists, from Aquinas onward, hold that moral content partly determines what counts as law. Ronald Dworkin argued that identifying the law requires interpreting legal practice in its best moral light, and pointed to disagreements among lawyers as evidence against positivism. Lon Fuller's parable of the inept King Rex illustrated formal requirements, often called the rule of law, that a system must meet to count as law at all.\n\nA further question is law's normativity: why legal requirements give anyone reasons to act. Command theorists appeal to fear of sanctions; Hart argued that officials treat rules as justified standards; Joseph Raz argued that law claims legitimate authority, which is justified when following it helps people act better on reasons they already have. Punishment, its justification and its social function, is a related field, which Foucault approached historically in Discipline and Punish.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/lawphil-nature/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" },
  { id: "mathematics", n: "Philosophy of mathematics", k: "branch", d: "The study of what mathematical objects are, how mathematical knowledge is possible, and how mathematics relates to logic.", q: ["Do numbers exist?", "How do we know mathematical truths?", "Can mathematics be reduced to logic?"], p: ["plato", "kant", "frege", "russell", "godel"], st: ["Frege, The Foundations of Arithmetic (1884)", "Russell, Introduction to Mathematical Philosophy (1919)"], rel: ["logic", "analytic", "science"], x: "Philosophy of mathematics asks what mathematical objects are and how we can know about them, given that numbers and sets seem not to be physical and mathematical knowledge seems to come from proof alone. Around 1900 three programs tried to secure the foundations of mathematics. Logicism, from Frege and Russell, sought to reduce mathematics to logic; Frege's system fell to Russell's paradox, though a neo-logicism built on Hume's principle has been revived. Intuitionism, from Brouwer, treats mathematics as mental construction, rejects completed infinities and proofs of existence that give no construction, and so revises classical logic. Formalism, from Hilbert, treated higher mathematics as a formal system whose consistency was to be proved; Gödel's incompleteness theorems undermined that hope.\n\nPlatonists hold that mathematical objects exist independently of us. Gödel defended this view, and Quine and Putnam argued that, because our best scientific theories cannot do without mathematics, we are committed to the entities it describes. Paul Benacerraf posed two problems for platonism: how we could know about abstract objects that we cannot causally interact with, and which sets the natural numbers really are, when several set-theoretic constructions serve equally well.\n\nStructuralists such as Shapiro and Resnik answer that mathematics describes structures, positions in patterns, and nominalists, such as Hartry Field, try to do without abstract objects altogether, or treat mathematics as a kind of useful fiction. Current debates concern whether set theory or category theory should serve as the foundation, the status of the continuum hypothesis, computer-assisted proofs such as that of the four-color theorem, and the practice of mathematics as it actually develops.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/philosophy-mathematics/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" },
  // traditions and approaches
  { id: "ancient", n: "Ancient Greek and Roman philosophy", k: "tradition", d: "From the Presocratics through Socrates, Plato and Aristotle to the Hellenistic schools (Stoics, Epicureans, Skeptics). It set many of the questions and terms the later Western tradition works with.", q: ["What is the underlying nature of things?", "What is virtue, and can it be taught?", "How should one live to be free of disturbance?"], p: ["parmenides", "socrates", "plato", "aristotle", "epicurus", "epictetus", "lucretius", "pyrrho", "sextus"], st: ["Plato, Republic", "Aristotle, Nicomachean Ethics", "Epictetus, Handbook", "Lucretius, On the Nature of Things"], rel: ["islamic", "german-idealism", "metaphysics", "ethics"], x: "Greek philosophy began in the sixth century BCE with thinkers who sought reasoned explanations of nature in place of myth. Thales proposed water as the basic stuff of things, Anaximander an unlimited and indefinite substrate, Anaximenes air. Heraclitus held that all things are in flux and governed by a logos, Parmenides that what is is one and unchanging, and Zeno defended him with paradoxes of motion. Empedocles and Anaxagoras posited many elements, and Leucippus and Democritus atoms moving in the void. The Sophists taught rhetoric for pay, and Protagoras declared that man is the measure of all things.\n\nSocrates turned philosophy toward ethics and the conduct of life, questioning his fellow citizens until they reached perplexity; he held that virtue is knowledge, and Athens executed him for impiety in 399 BCE. Plato's dialogues, most of them led by Socrates, set out the theory of Forms, the immortality of the soul, and in the Republic a city ruled by philosopher-kings. Aristotle, Plato's student for twenty years, founded the Lyceum, criticized the Forms, and wrote on logic, physics, biology, ethics, politics and rhetoric.\n\nAfter Alexander the Hellenistic schools offered ways of life. Cynics such as Diogenes rejected convention; Epicureans joined atomism to the pursuit of freedom from pain and fear; Stoics taught living in agreement with nature and reason and developed a powerful logic; Pyrrhonists and Academic skeptics suspended judgment. Neoplatonism, with Plotinus in the third century CE, closed the period. Greek texts, preserved in Byzantium and taken up by al-Kindī, al-Fārābī, Ibn Sīnā and Ibn Rushd, returned to Latin Europe through translation in the twelfth and thirteenth centuries.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ancient_Greek_philosophy", srcName: "Wikipedia" },
  { id: "german-idealism", n: "German idealism", k: "tradition", d: "The philosophy that developed in response to Kant's critical philosophy, from Fichte and Schelling to Hegel's system, centered on reason, freedom and self-consciousness.", q: ["What are the conditions of possible experience?", "How is self-consciousness structured?", "Can philosophy grasp the whole as a system?"], p: ["kant", "fichte", "schelling", "hegel"], st: ["Kant, Critique of Pure Reason (1781)", "Hegel, Phenomenology of Spirit (1807)"], rel: ["marxism", "phenomenology", "critical-theory", "history"], x: "German idealism ran from about the 1780s to the 1840s, and its main figures were Kant, Fichte, Schelling and Hegel. Kant held that we know objects only as they appear to us, under the forms of our sensibility and understanding, never as they are in themselves. Early critics read this as a version of Berkeley's idealism, a reading Kant rejected.\n\nReinhold tried to derive Kant's philosophy from a single principle of consciousness. Fichte recast that first principle as an act in which the I posits itself and, in doing so, a not-I opposed to it, and he argued that the idea of a thing in itself is incoherent. Schelling began as Fichte's follower, developed a philosophy of nature, and came to hold that subject and object are identical in an absolute that can be approached from either side. Hegel called Fichte's idealism subjective and Schelling's objective, and sought a position beyond both; his Phenomenology of Spirit (1807) traces consciousness through a series of shapes toward absolute knowing, and his dialectical logic underlies his whole system.\n\nThe movement aimed at a system covering logic, metaphysics, nature, ethics, politics, art and religion, and it gave freedom and self-consciousness a central place. It also gave art a high role in grasping the absolute, though Hegel later held that art had lost its highest vocation. After Hegel's death his followers split into Left and Right Hegelians over the politics of his system, and Schopenhauer, Kierkegaard and Marx attacked it. Its influence faded with the rise of positivism and neo-Kantianism, then revived in the twentieth and twenty-first centuries, in analytic as well as continental philosophy.", src: "https://iep.utm.edu/germidea/", srcName: "Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy" },
  { id: "phenomenology", n: "Phenomenology, hermeneutics and existentialism", k: "tradition", d: "Phenomenology describes the structures of lived experience. Hermeneutics studies interpretation and understanding. Existentialism centers on freedom, choice and concrete existence.", q: ["How is the world given in experience?", "What is it to understand a text, or a life?", "What does freedom demand of us?"], p: ["kierkegaard", "husserl", "heidegger", "sartre", "beauvoir", "merleauponty", "levinas", "gadamer", "ricoeur"], st: ["Husserl, Ideas I (1913)", "Heidegger, Being and Time (1927)", "Sartre, Being and Nothingness (1943)", "Merleau-Ponty, Phenomenology of Perception (1945)", "Gadamer, Truth and Method (1960)"], rel: ["german-idealism", "poststructuralism", "mind", "race"], x: "Phenomenology is the study of the structures of conscious experience as lived from the first-person point of view. Its central concept is intentionality: experience is always of or about something, presented through a meaning. Brentano called the study of mental phenomena descriptive psychology, and Husserl made it a discipline of its own in the Logical Investigations (1900-01) and Ideas I (1913). His method of epoché, or bracketing, sets aside the question whether the world exists in order to describe how things show up in experience. Heidegger's Being and Time (1927) shifted attention to practical involvement in a world and to the question of the meaning of being; Sartre developed an account of consciousness and freedom, and Merleau-Ponty one of the lived body and perception.\n\nHermeneutics is the theory of interpretation. Schleiermacher proposed a general art of interpreting any text, and Dilthey grounded the human sciences in understanding. Heidegger made interpretation a basic feature of human existence. Gadamer's Truth and Method (1960) argued that prejudices, in the sense of prior judgments handed down by tradition, make understanding possible, that we are shaped by an effective history we can never fully survey, and that understanding happens as a fusion of horizons. Habermas objected that this leaves too little ground for criticizing ideology; Ricoeur took a long route to self-understanding through symbol, metaphor and narrative.\n\nExistentialism, a loose movement that flourished in postwar France with roots in Kierkegaard and Nietzsche, starts from concrete individual existence. Only Sartre and Beauvoir accepted the label. Its themes include Sartre's slogan that existence precedes essence, so that people make themselves by their choices; freedom limited by facticity, the conditions one is born into; anxiety; authenticity against conformity to the crowd; and bad faith, the self-deception by which we flee our freedom.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/phenomenology/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hermeneutics/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Hermeneutics”" }, { src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/existentialism/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Existentialism”" }] },
  { id: "marxism", n: "Marxism", k: "tradition", d: "The tradition founded by Marx and Engels: a materialist account of history, a critique of political economy, and a theory of class struggle, developed later by Lukács, Gramsci, Althusser and many others.", q: ["How does capitalism produce and reproduce itself?", "How do ideas relate to material conditions?", "What would emancipation require?"], p: ["marx", "engels", "lukacs", "gramsci", "althusser", "williams"], st: ["Marx and Engels, The Communist Manifesto (1848)", "Marx, Capital, vol. 1 (1867)", "Lukács, History and Class Consciousness (1923)"], rel: ["german-idealism", "critical-theory", "feminist", "race", "postcolonial"], x: "Marxism is the method of social analysis and political philosophy founded by Karl Marx and Friedrich Engels in the nineteenth century. Historical materialism treats the way a society produces its means of life as its base, on which law, politics, culture and ideology arise as a superstructure. The Communist Manifesto (1848) opens with the claim that the history of all society so far is the history of class struggles.\n\nUnder capitalism, on Marx's account, those who own the means of production profit from the surplus value workers produce beyond what they are paid. Workers are alienated from their activity, from its products, from one another and from their own human capacities. Marx argued that capitalism's internal contradictions would lead to proletarian revolution, then to socialism and finally a classless and stateless communist society.\n\nLater Marxism divided into many currents. Marxism-Leninism became the official doctrine of the Soviet Union and other socialist states. Western Marxism, including Lukács, Gramsci and the Frankfurt School, turned to culture, consciousness and philosophy; Althusser developed a structural Marxism; Marxist humanists and Marxist feminists offered further revisions, many of them critical of state socialism. Marxist concepts shaped sociology, economics, history and literary criticism. Critics, among them Karl Popper and economists of the Austrian school, argue that Marx's predictions failed and that the labor theory of value is flawed, and point to the authoritarian states that claimed his name.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marxism", srcName: "Wikipedia" },
  { id: "critical-theory", n: "Frankfurt School critical theory", k: "tradition", d: "Social philosophy from the Institute for Social Research in Frankfurt, combining Marx, Hegel and Freud to criticize modern society with emancipatory aims.", q: ["Why did the promise of enlightenment turn into domination?", "How does mass culture shape consciousness?", "What can critique do?"], p: ["horkheimer", "adorno", "marcuse", "benjamin", "habermas", "honneth"], st: ["Horkheimer, “Traditional and Critical Theory” (1937)", "Horkheimer and Adorno, Dialectic of Enlightenment (1944/1947)", "Marcuse, One-Dimensional Man (1964)"], rel: ["marxism", "german-idealism", "aesthetics", "poststructuralism"], x: "In its narrow sense, critical theory is the work of the Frankfurt School, the circle around the Institute for Social Research founded in Frankfurt in 1923. In a broader sense the name covers emancipatory theories tied to social movements, such as feminist, critical race, queer and postcolonial theory. Horkheimer's inaugural lecture of 1931 set out an interdisciplinary program joining philosophy with social theory, psychology, political economy and cultural analysis, and his essay “Traditional and Critical Theory” (1937) defined critical theory as theory that questions the existing social order and aims at human emancipation. The school drew on Marx, Freud, Weber, Lukács, Kant, Hegel and Nietzsche.\n\nThe first generation included Horkheimer, Adorno, Marcuse and Benjamin. They studied the psychological roots of authoritarianism, culminating in The Authoritarian Personality (1950). Horkheimer and Adorno's Dialectic of Enlightenment (1947) argued that instrumental reason turns enlightenment into new forms of domination and that the culture industry standardizes culture into commodities; Benjamin was more hopeful about mass-reproduced art. Marcuse's One-Dimensional Man (1964) argued that advanced capitalism absorbs dissent. Adorno developed a negative dialectics attentive to what escapes concepts, and defended autonomous art as a form of resistance.\n\nHabermas, leading the second generation, grounded critique in communicative reason: the lifeworld of shared meanings is distorted when money and administrative power colonize it, and in his discourse ethics valid norms are those all affected could accept in rational discussion. Honneth's theory of recognition, building on Hegel, and the work of Benhabib, Fraser and others mark the third generation, which has taken the tradition toward feminist and anti-colonial concerns.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/critical-theory/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" },
  { id: "poststructuralism", n: "Structuralism and post-structuralism", k: "tradition", d: "Structuralism explains meaning through systems of differences, from Saussure's linguistics to Lévi-Strauss's anthropology. Post-structuralism, a largely English-language label, covers French thinkers who questioned stable structures, origins and the unified subject.", q: ["How do systems of difference produce meaning?", "How are subjects formed by language, discourse and power?", "What do texts exclude in order to cohere?"], p: ["saussure", "levistrauss", "lacan", "barthes", "althusser", "foucault", "derrida", "kristeva", "lyotard"], st: ["Saussure, Course in General Linguistics (1916)", "Barthes, Mythologies (1957)", "Derrida, Of Grammatology (1967)", "Foucault, Discipline and Punish (1975)"], rel: ["phenomenology", "marxism", "feminist", "postcolonial", "language"], x: "Structuralism explained meaning through systems of relations. Saussure's Course in General Linguistics (1916), compiled from his lectures after his death, treated language as a system of signs whose elements gain their value from their differences from one another, and in the 1950s and 1960s Lévi-Strauss in anthropology, Barthes in literary and cultural criticism, Lacan in psychoanalysis and Althusser in Marxism extended the model.\n\nPost-structuralism emerged in 1960s France from a close and conflicted relation to structuralism. It questions the stability and objectivity of the structures structuralism posited, treats them as shaped by history and by power, and interrogates the binary oppositions, such as speech and writing or reason and emotion, on which analyses rely. Derrida's lecture “Structure, Sign, and Play in the Discourse of the Human Sciences” (1966) announced a decentering of the structures of thought, and Barthes's essay “The Death of the Author” (1967) argued that a text's meaning is produced in reading and is not fixed by its author's intention.\n\nWriters often labeled post-structuralist include Barthes, Derrida, Foucault, Deleuze and Baudrillard, many of whom rejected the label. A common thread is that knowledge cannot rest on pure experience or on timeless structures, since the conditions of history and culture shape how any structure is studied. The movement has drawn sharp criticism for its style and rigor, from John Searle and from Alan Sokal among others.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Post-structuralism", srcName: "Wikipedia" },
  { id: "feminist", n: "Feminist philosophy", k: "tradition", d: "Philosophy that examines gender, sexuality and the oppression of women, and how they shape knowledge, ethics, politics and the self.", q: ["How is gender produced and maintained?", "How does social position shape knowledge?", "What would gender justice require?"], p: ["wollstonecraft", "beauvoir", "hartsock", "harding", "haraway", "butler", "federici", "crenshaw", "fricker"], st: ["Wollstonecraft, A Vindication of the Rights of Woman (1792)", "Beauvoir, The Second Sex (1949)", "Butler, Gender Trouble (1990)"], rel: ["marxism", "race", "poststructuralism", "epistemology"], x: "Feminism is at once a political movement to end gender-based oppression and a body of concepts and theories for analyzing that oppression. Feminist philosophy brings this commitment to philosophy. Concern for justice for women is much older than any organized movement, and the field's history runs through Mary Astell and Mary Wollstonecraft to Simone de Beauvoir's The Second Sex (1949). Contemporary feminist philosophy emerged in the 1970s, as more women entered universities, and built its own institutions when mainstream journals were slow to accept its work: the Society for Women in Philosophy (1972) and the journal Hypatia (1982) among them.\n\nFeminist philosophers generally treat people as embodied and socially situated, distrust claims to neutrality or universality that may reflect a particular, usually male, standpoint, and study how gender shapes norms and has been used to exclude or subordinate. They work across the discipline: in epistemology and philosophy of science, ethics, political philosophy, metaphysics, aesthetics, philosophy of law, religion and language, and the history of philosophy. Analytic, continental, pragmatist, psychoanalytic and Latin American approaches differ in style and method.\n\nThe distinction between sex and gender has been central to much analytic feminism. Continental feminists have questioned it from two directions: Judith Butler by asking whether sex is itself socially constituted, Luce Irigaray and Hélène Cixous by theorizing sexual difference. Feminist philosophers have also stressed that gender interacts with race, class, disability and sexuality, a point Kimberlé Crenshaw named intersectionality, and debates over defining “woman” now extend to trans and nonbinary people.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/feminist-philosophy/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" },
  { id: "race", n: "Philosophy of race and critical race theory", k: "tradition", d: "Inquiry into what race is, how racial categories are made and enforced, and how racism structures law, knowledge and political economy.", q: ["What kind of thing is race?", "How is racism built into institutions?", "How do race, class and gender interlock?"], p: ["dubois", "fanon", "bell", "crenshaw", "omi", "winant", "robinson", "mills"], st: ["Du Bois, The Souls of Black Folk (1903)", "Fanon, Black Skin, White Masks (1952)", "Mills, The Racial Contract (1997)"], rel: ["feminist", "postcolonial", "marxism", "african"], x: "The traditional concept of race divided humanity into a few groups defined by inherited biology, with sharp boundaries, geographical origins and physical, sometimes behavioral, markers. Most scholars treat it as a modern concept, though some find earlier versions in medieval Europe and in fifteenth-century Spanish laws of blood purity. Philosophers including Hume and Kant, and naturalists such as Blumenbach, contributed to its early modern classifications. In the twentieth century Franz Boas challenged the fixity of racial traits, and UNESCO's statement of 1950 rejected biological race.\n\nContemporary philosophers divide over whether races exist. Racial naturalism, the old biological view, is now rejected. Skeptics such as Kwame Anthony Appiah and Naomi Zack argued that there are no races and the concept should be eliminated. Constructivists, such as Sally Haslanger, hold that races are real because societies create and sustain them through social and institutional practices. Others defend minimal or population-based conceptions, while critics note that drawing those population boundaries still involves human decisions. Further debates concern the difference between race and ethnicity, what racism is, racial identity and solidarity, and policy questions such as reparations, affirmative action and segregation.\n\nCritical race theory arose in American legal scholarship in the 1970s and 1980s, as civil rights gains were being eroded. Derrick Bell, Alan Freeman, Kimberlé Crenshaw, Richard Delgado, Cheryl Harris, Mari Matsuda and Patricia Williams argued that racism is built into laws and institutions as well as individual prejudice. Its ideas include Bell's interest convergence, Crenshaw's intersectionality, Harris's “whiteness as property” and the use of counter-stories. Since 2020 it has also been the subject of political controversy in the United States.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/race/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", srcs: [{ src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Critical_race_theory", srcName: "Wikipedia, “Critical race theory”" }] },
  { id: "postcolonial", n: "Postcolonial and decolonial thought", k: "tradition", d: "Thought about colonialism and its aftermath: how empire shaped knowledge, culture and identity, and what liberation from it involves.", q: ["How did colonialism shape what counts as knowledge?", "Who can speak for the colonized?", "What does decolonization require?"], p: ["cesaire", "fanon", "said", "guha", "spivak", "mbembe"], st: ["Césaire, Discourse on Colonialism (1950)", "Fanon, The Wretched of the Earth (1961)", "Said, Orientalism (1978)", "Spivak, “Can the Subaltern Speak?” (1988)"], rel: ["race", "marxism", "poststructuralism", "african"], x: "Colonialism is a practice of domination in which one people is subjected to another. The term is often used for European settlement, dispossession and rule, and imperialism for domination without large-scale settlement, though the two are often interchanged. Spanish conquest in the Americas was justified by religious mission and papal authority, and challenged on natural-law grounds by Bartolomé de las Casas and Francisco de Vitoria, though Vitoria still allowed force under some conditions. Liberal thinkers such as John Stuart Mill defended despotic rule over peoples they ranked lower on a scale of civilization, and Tocqueville supported French rule in Algeria.\n\nMarx saw British rule in India as destructive but historically progressive; Lenin treated imperialism as a stage of capitalism driven by competition for markets, a line of analysis extended by Luxemburg and later by Wallerstein's world-systems theory. Anti-colonial thinkers wrote from within the struggle. Frantz Fanon analyzed colonial violence, its psychological effects, and the national bourgeoisie that inherits power; Gandhi developed satyagraha and a critique of Western civilization.\n\nPostcolonial theory, especially in literary and cultural studies, grew from Edward Said's Orientalism, which examined how Western knowledge about “the Orient” served power, and from Gayatri Spivak's “Can the Subaltern Speak?”, which asked whether the most marginalized can be represented without being spoken for. Scholars of settler colonialism, many of them Indigenous, such as Glen Coulthard and Leanne Simpson, argue that the language of the postcolonial obscures colonial rule that continues, criticize politics of recognition and reconciliation, and emphasize Indigenous self-determination.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/colonialism/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" },
  { id: "pragmatism", n: "Pragmatism", k: "tradition", d: "An American tradition that understands meaning and truth through practical consequences and inquiry.", q: ["What difference does a belief make in practice?", "How are truth and successful practice related?", "How should inquiry proceed?"], p: ["peirce", "james", "dewey"], st: ["Peirce, “How to Make Our Ideas Clear” (1878)", "James, Pragmatism (1907)", "Dewey, Experience and Nature (1925)"], rel: ["analytic", "epistemology", "science"], x: "Pragmatism treats knowing the world as inseparable from acting in it. It began in the United States around 1870, in discussions at the Metaphysical Club in Cambridge, Massachusetts. Charles Sanders Peirce gave it its method in 1878, the pragmatic maxim: to make a concept clear, trace the practical consequences of its applying. He held that inquiry is always fallible and that truth is what inquiry would arrive at if pursued far enough, and he later renamed his view “pragmaticism” to separate it from James's.\n\nWilliam James popularized pragmatism in 1907, using it to settle or dissolve disputes such as that between free will and determinism, and treated truth as what works in experience, a view critics found too permissive. John Dewey understood inquiry as the response to a problematic situation, and connected it to education, democracy and art. Jane Addams, W. E. B. Du Bois, Alain Locke and George Herbert Mead carried pragmatist ideas into social reform, race, value and the social self.\n\nFrom the 1970s neo-pragmatists such as Richard Rorty, Hilary Putnam, Robert Brandom and Jürgen Habermas revived the tradition, often through language and anti-representationalism, and new pragmatists such as Cheryl Misak sought to restore its classical concern for objectivity. Common themes are fallibilism in place of skepticism, concepts and theories treated as tools judged by their results, and suspicion of sharp dichotomies such as fact and value or theory and practice.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/pragmatism/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" },
  { id: "analytic", n: "Analytic philosophy", k: "tradition", d: "The tradition, dominant in English-speaking universities, that began with logic and the analysis of language, and prizes argumentative clarity.", q: ["What do our sentences really say?", "Can philosophical problems be solved by clarifying language?", "How does philosophy relate to science?"], p: ["frege", "russell", "moore", "wittgenstein", "carnap", "quine"], st: ["Russell, “On Denoting” (1905)", "Wittgenstein, Tractatus Logico-Philosophicus (1921)", "Quine, “Two Dogmas of Empiricism” (1951)"], rel: ["logic", "language", "pragmatism", "science"], x: "Analytic philosophy is usually dated from the moment, around 1900, when G. E. Moore and Bertrand Russell rejected British idealism as obscure. Frege, whose logic and theory of sense and reference it built on, is often called its father, and Bolzano, Brentano, Meinong and the early Husserl are counted among its precursors. Moore defended common-sense beliefs against the idealists' claim that they concern mere appearance, and Russell's theory of descriptions became a model of philosophical analysis. Wittgenstein's Tractatus (1921) and Russell's logical atomism held that the world consists of facts that language can picture once its logical form is revealed.\n\nIn the 1920s and 1930s the logical positivists of the Vienna and Berlin Circles, among them Schlick, Carnap, Neurath, Reichenbach and Hempel, held that a statement is meaningful only if it is true by definition or verifiable by experience, which left metaphysics, theology, ethics and aesthetics without cognitive meaning. Emigration after 1933 carried the movement to Britain and the United States, and Ayer's Language, Truth and Logic (1936) introduced it to English readers. At Oxford, ordinary language philosophers such as Ryle, Strawson and J. L. Austin turned to everyday usage, and the later Wittgenstein tied meaning to use.\n\nQuine's attack on the analytic-synthetic distinction weakened positivism, and Kripke's Naming and Necessity helped revive essence and necessity as subjects, so that metaphysics returned to the center of the tradition. Ethics, which many early analytic philosophers thought could not be made rigorous, became a major field, divided into metaethics, normative ethics and applied ethics. The tradition is now marked chiefly by its methods: clear prose, rigorous argument, formal logic, attention to the natural sciences, and close attention to language and meaning.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Analytic_philosophy", srcName: "Wikipedia" },
  { id: "african", n: "African philosophy", k: "tradition", d: "Philosophy from African traditions and thinkers, including debates over what African philosophy is, and work on personhood, community and knowledge.", q: ["What makes a philosophy African?", "What is a person, and what does a person owe the community?", "How should philosophy relate to traditional thought?"], p: ["tempels", "senghor", "hountondji", "wiredu", "oruka", "gyekye", "menkiti"], st: ["Hountondji, African Philosophy: Myth and Reality (1976)", "Wiredu, Philosophy and an African Culture (1980)", "Gyekye, An Essay on African Philosophical Thought (1987)"], rel: ["postcolonial", "race", "ethics"], x: "African philosophy draws on African systems of thought and addresses metaphysics, knowledge, ethics and politics. Before the twentieth century much of it was transmitted orally. Written sources include ancient Egyptian wisdom literature such as the teachings of Ptahhotep and the concept of maat, associated with truth and justice; Islamic scholarship in the medieval kingdoms of Ghana, Mali and Songhai; and the seventeenth-century Ethiopian philosopher Zera Yacob and his student Walda Heywat.\n\nTwentieth-century debate turned on what African philosophy is. Placide Tempels's Bantu Philosophy (1945) presented the shared worldview of a people as a philosophy, an approach later called ethnophilosophy. Paulin Hountondji criticized it for confusing collective beliefs with the critical, individual activity of philosophy. Henry Odera Oruka distinguished several trends, including sage philosophy, the reflective thought of individual wise persons in traditional communities, and professional philosophy written by academically trained philosophers. Léopold Senghor's négritude and the thought of political leaders such as Kwame Nkrumah and Julius Nyerere formed a nationalist-ideological strand.\n\nRecurring themes include personhood and community, summed up in the Nguni concept of ubuntu, often rendered “I am because we are”; an ethics centered on character and on duties to others; and the question of how much weight duties to the community should carry against individual rights. Kwasi Wiredu argued that critical, independent thought was already practized in Akan communities, in long disputations among elders aimed at consensus, and called for the conceptual decolonization of African philosophy, a careful examination of concepts absorbed through colonial languages.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/African_philosophy", srcName: "Wikipedia", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/african-sage/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “African Sage Philosophy”" }] },
  { id: "chinese", n: "Chinese philosophy", k: "tradition", d: "Confucian, Daoist, Mohist, Legalist, Buddhist and Neo-Confucian thought, much of it concerned with ethics, government and harmony with the dao, or way.", q: ["How does one become a good person?", "What is the dao?", "How should a ruler govern?"], p: ["confucius", "mencius", "xunzi", "laozi", "zhuangzi"], st: ["Confucius, Analects", "Laozi, Daodejing", "Zhuangzi"], rel: ["ethics", "metaphysics"], x: "Chinese philosophy took shape in the Spring and Autumn and Warring States periods (about 770-221 BCE), when the rival “Hundred Schools of Thought” competed for the attention of rulers. The four most influential were Confucianism, Daoism, Mohism and Legalism. Confucius and his followers Mencius and Xunzi taught that a good society rests on cultivated persons and ritual propriety, and argued over whether human nature is good or bad. Daoists such as Laozi and Zhuangzi pointed to the dao, the way of things, and to effortless action. Mozi argued for impartial care, and the Legalists, above all Han Feizi, for strict laws and state power.\n\nUnder the Han dynasty Confucianism became the state ideology, combined with the theories of yin and yang and the five phases. Buddhism, arriving from India, grew into a major intellectual force and produced Chinese schools such as Chan, known in Japan as Zen; Daoism also developed into an organized religion. In the Song and Ming dynasties Neo-Confucians reshaped the tradition: Zhu Xi emphasized li, the principle or pattern in all things, and Wang Yangming the innate moral knowledge of the mind and the unity of knowledge and action.\n\nMany Chinese schools share an emphasis on ethical and political guidance, a reluctance to set humans apart from nature, and concepts such as dao, de (virtue or power), li (ritual), qi (vital stuff) and yin and yang. Modern Chinese philosophy engaged with Western thought from the late Qing dynasty, and after 1949 Marxism became official doctrine; since the 1980s New Confucianism has revived classical themes.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_philosophy", srcName: "Wikipedia" },
  { id: "indian", n: "Indian philosophy", k: "tradition", d: "The classical Indian schools, Hindu, Buddhist and Jain, which debated the self, consciousness, knowledge and liberation with highly developed epistemology and logic.", q: ["Is there an enduring self?", "What are the valid sources of knowledge?", "What is liberation, and how is it reached?"], p: ["nagarjuna", "shankara", "ramanuja"], st: ["The Upanishads", "The Bhagavad Gītā", "Nāgārjuna, Mūlamadhyamakakārikā"], rel: ["metaphysics", "religion", "logic"], x: "The philosophical traditions of the Indian subcontinent are often called darśanas, “views”. They are commonly divided into āstika schools, which accept the authority of the Vedas, and nāstika schools, which do not, though the criteria are disputed. The six āstika schools are Nyāya (logic and the theory of knowledge), Vaiśeṣika (categories and atoms), Sāṃkhya (a dualism of consciousness and matter), Yoga (the discipline of meditation), Mīmāṃsā (the interpretation of Vedic ritual), and Vedānta (the teaching of the Upaniṣads on ātman, the self, and brahman, ultimate reality). The nāstika schools include Buddhism, Jainism, the Ājīvikas and the materialist Cārvākas.\n\nMost schools share the concepts of karma, rebirth (saṃsāra) and dharma, and aim at liberation (mokṣa or nirvāṇa), but disagree sharply about the self and the nature of reality. They developed elaborate theories of the valid means of knowledge (pramāṇas), such as perception, inference and testimony. Buddhists deny any permanent self; Nāgārjuna's Madhyamaka teaches the emptiness of all things, and Yogācāra holds that only consciousness is real. Jains teach non-violence (ahiṃsā) and the many-sidedness of truth (anekāntavāda). Within Vedānta, Śaṅkara's Advaita holds that self and brahman are not two, and Rāmānuja's Viśiṣṭādvaita that the world and selves are real attributes of brahman.\n\nThe early Upaniṣads date to roughly 1000-500 BCE, and the main schools took shape between about 500 BCE and the late first millennium CE. Indian thought later influenced Schopenhauer and the American Transcendentalists, and Gandhi made ahiṃsā and satyāgraha instruments of the independence movement.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indian_philosophy", srcName: "Wikipedia" },
  { id: "islamic", n: "Philosophy in the Islamic world", k: "tradition", d: "Philosophy written mainly in Arabic and Persian that took up and transformed Greek thought, debating God, the intellect and the relation of reason to revelation.", q: ["How do reason and revelation relate?", "Is the world eternal or created?", "What is the intellect?"], p: ["farabi", "ibnsina", "ghazali", "ibnrushd"], st: ["Ibn Sīnā, The Book of Healing", "al-Ghazālī, The Incoherence of the Philosophers", "Ibn Rushd, The Incoherence of the Incoherence"], rel: ["ancient", "religion", "metaphysics"], x: "Philosophy in the Islamic world developed along two main currents. Falsafa, from the Greek philosophia, took up Aristotle and the Neoplatonists in Arabic translation and also covered logic, mathematics and natural science; kalām, rational theology, included the Muʿtazilī, Ashʿarī and Māturīdī schools. Many scholars prefer the name “Arabic philosophy”, since its practitioners included Christians and Jews and its subjects included logic, medicine and natural science as well as religion. Free will against predestination was a central early debate.\n\nThe classical period runs from al-Kindī in the ninth century to Ibn Rushd at the end of the twelfth. Al-Kindī wrote the first original works of logic in Arabic and al-Fārābī developed logic and political philosophy. Ibn Sīnā distinguished essence from existence, built his own logic, and devised the thought experiment of the floating man to show the soul's awareness of itself. Al-Ghazālī's Incoherence of the Philosophers attacked the philosophers' views on causation and the eternity of the world, and Ibn Rushd defended philosophy against him. Ibn Khaldūn's Muqaddimah is regarded as a founding work of the philosophy of history and social theory.\n\nAfter Ibn Rushd, philosophy declined in the Islamic west but continued in the east, in the illuminationist school of Suhrawardī, in Sufi philosophy, and in the School of Isfahan under the Safavids, where Mulla Sadra developed his transcendent philosophy. Latin translations of Arabic works shaped medieval European natural philosophy, psychology and metaphysics, and Ibn Rushd's commentaries on Aristotle influenced Jewish and Christian scholastics. A revival came with the Nahda of the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Islamic_philosophy", srcName: "Wikipedia" },
];
